lName":"Lenovo Center","address":{"city":"Raleigh","state":"NC","country":"USA"},"grass":false,"images":[{"href":"https://a.espncdn.com/i/venues/nhl/day/999.jpg","width":2000,"height":1125,"alt":"","rel":["full","day"]}]},"attendance":18828,"officials":[{"fullName":"Jonny Murray","displayName":"Jonny Murray","position":{"name":"Linesman","displayName":"Linesman","id":"25"},"order":0},{"fullName":"TJ Luxmore","displayName":"TJ Luxmore","position":{"name":"Referee","displayName":"Referee","id":"24"},"order":0},{"fullName":"Brandon Blandina","displayName":"Brandon Blandina","position":{"name":"Referee","displayName":"Referee","id":"24"},"order":0},{"fullName":"Julien Fournier","displayName":"Julien Fournier","position":{"name":"Linesman","displayName":"Linesman","id":"25"},"order":0}]},"article":{"headline":"Svechnikov scores 2 goals to lead the Hurricanes to a 4-2 win over the Capitals","description":"— Andrei Svechnikov scored two goals, including the winner with 6:04 left in the third period, to lead the Carolina Hurricanes to a 4-2 win over the Washington Capitals on Tuesday night.","story":"RALEIGH, N.C. -- — <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/4352683/andrei-svechnikov\">Andrei Svechnikov</a> scored two goals, including the winner with 6:04 left in the third period, to lead the <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/team/_/name/car/carolina-hurricanes\">Carolina Hurricanes</a> to a 4-2 win over the <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/team/_/name/wsh/washington-capitals\">Washington Capitals</a> on Tuesday night.\n\n\r\n              <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/4319879/eric-robinson\">Eric Robinson</a> and <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/2976849/mark-jankowski\">Mark Jankowski</a> also scored for the Hurricanes, who have won six straight. Goalie <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/4565259/pyotr-kochetkov\">Pyotr Kochetkov</a> made 20 saves and improved to 4-0-0 this season.\n\n\r\n              <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/2563054/boone-jenner\">Boone Jenner</a> and <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/5763/justin-holl\">Justin Holl</a> scored for Washington. <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/5149172/ryan-leonard\">Ryan Leonard</a> added two assists and <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/4272888/logan-thompson\">Logan Thompson</a> stopped 31 shots.\n\n\r\n              Svechnikov beat Thompson with a backhander six seconds after <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/4697783/justin-sourdif\">Justin Sourdif</a> went to the penalty box for tripping. The Hurricanes finished 1 for 5 on the power play.\n\n\r\n              It was the last scheduled visit to Raleigh for Capitals captain <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/3101/alex-ovechkin\">Alex Ovechkin</a>, who received a standing ovation early in the first period.\n\n\r\n              The Lenovo Center has been a home away from home for the 41-year-old Russian, who is set to retire after this season. He was the No. 1 pick in the 2004 NHL draft held in Raleigh and his 59 career points in 49 regular-season games are the most he has recorded at any NHL venue other than Capital One Arena in Washington.\n\n\r\n              The Hurricanes, who were without captain <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/3541/jordan-staal\">Jordan Staal</a>, were able to keep Ovechkin in check on Tuesday but Jenner remained hot. One of Washington’s top free-agent signings in the offseason, Jenner has scored in three straight games. He gave the Capitals a 1-0 lead at 9:26 in the first period.\n\n\r\n              Svechnikov, who has six goals in the past five games, tied the game at 1-1 by poking in a rebound of <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/3042054/william-carrier\">William Carrier</a>'s shot 3:58 into the second period.\n\n\r\n              Holl’s shot from the right circle with 6:39 left in the second period gave Washington a 2-1 lead.\n\n\r\n              Carolina tied it at 2 with 1:11 left in the second when Robinson’s pass deflected off of Martin Fehervary’s stick past Thompson.\n\n\r\n              Earlier on Tuesday, the Hurricanes announced that Staal would miss at least a month with a calf injury. Coach Rod Brind’Amour said that the 38-year-old center got hurt in warmups before Sunday’s 4-1 win at Philadelphia.\n\n\r\n              <hl2>Up next</hl2>\r\n              Capitals: Host Montreal on Wednesday.\n\n\r\n              Hurricanes: Begin a five-game road trip at Winnipeg on Saturday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                <a href=\"https://apnews.com/hub/nhl\">See AP’s full NHL coverage here</a>","published":"2026-10-14T01:38:41Z"}}